{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-8352" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is **NOT** a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7543c24749d210763776b1dd36828a705b7ca0c0e31051b46dad1ebd313139c"} {"question" "q-8352" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x{y}^{-1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation cannot be written in standard form since the power of $y$ in the term $x{y}^{-1}$ is not $1.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7543c24749d210763776b1dd36828a705b7ca0c0e31051b46dad1ebd313139c"} {"question" "q-8352" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eb4d28ad52ee2323cc5eba3288092cabe973f18909117f96787114c62ba4296e"} {"question" "q-8352" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7543c24749d210763776b1dd36828a705b7ca0c0e31051b46dad1ebd313139c"} {"question" "q-8352" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{e}^{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=x"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=xy"] ["math" "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7543c24749d210763776b1dd36828a705b7ca0c0e31051b46dad1ebd313139c"} {"question" "q-8352" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7543c24749d210763776b1dd36828a705b7ca0c0e31051b46dad1ebd313139c"} {"question" "q-79752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is **NOT** a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7543c24749d210763776b1dd36828a705b7ca0c0e31051b46dad1ebd313139c"} {"question" "q-79752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\sqrt{y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\ncannot be written in standard form since the power of $y$ in the term $\\sqrt{y}$ is not $1.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7543c24749d210763776b1dd36828a705b7ca0c0e31051b46dad1ebd313139c"} {"question" "q-79752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eb4d28ad52ee2323cc5eba3288092cabe973f18909117f96787114c62ba4296e"} {"question" "q-79752" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7543c24749d210763776b1dd36828a705b7ca0c0e31051b46dad1ebd313139c"} {"question" "q-79752" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}=0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}={e}^{2x}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\sqrt{y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7543c24749d210763776b1dd36828a705b7ca0c0e31051b46dad1ebd313139c"} {"question" "q-79752" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\sqrt{y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7543c24749d210763776b1dd36828a705b7ca0c0e31051b46dad1ebd313139c"} {"question" "q-103551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7543c24749d210763776b1dd36828a705b7ca0c0e31051b46dad1ebd313139c"} {"question" "q-103551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nis already is written in standard form with  $P(x)=-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7543c24749d210763776b1dd36828a705b7ca0c0e31051b46dad1ebd313139c"} {"question" "q-103551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eb4d28ad52ee2323cc5eba3288092cabe973f18909117f96787114c62ba4296e"} {"question" "q-103551" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7543c24749d210763776b1dd36828a705b7ca0c0e31051b46dad1ebd313139c"} {"question" "q-103551" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy={x}^{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)=x-1"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=\\frac{1}{x}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{3}y=\\frac{x}{1+y}"] ["math" "{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+{x}^{2}y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7543c24749d210763776b1dd36828a705b7ca0c0e31051b46dad1ebd313139c"} {"question" "q-103551" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=\\frac{1}{x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7543c24749d210763776b1dd36828a705b7ca0c0e31051b46dad1ebd313139c"} {"question" "q-331729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+7xy=5$\n- ${y}^{′}+7xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+7xy=-2y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7543c24749d210763776b1dd36828a705b7ca0c0e31051b46dad1ebd313139c"} {"question" "q-331729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7xy=5.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $5$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7xy=-2y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+7xy & =-2y \\\\ {y}^{′}+7xy+2y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(7x+2)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7543c24749d210763776b1dd36828a705b7ca0c0e31051b46dad1ebd313139c"} {"question" "q-331729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eb4d28ad52ee2323cc5eba3288092cabe973f18909117f96787114c62ba4296e"} {"question" "q-331729" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7543c24749d210763776b1dd36828a705b7ca0c0e31051b46dad1ebd313139c"} {"question" "q-331729" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7543c24749d210763776b1dd36828a705b7ca0c0e31051b46dad1ebd313139c"} {"question" "q-331729" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7543c24749d210763776b1dd36828a705b7ca0c0e31051b46dad1ebd313139c"} {"question" "q-331744" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=y$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy={x}^{3}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7543c24749d210763776b1dd36828a705b7ca0c0e31051b46dad1ebd313139c"} {"question" "q-331744" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x+1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy={x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)={x}^{3}$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7543c24749d210763776b1dd36828a705b7ca0c0e31051b46dad1ebd313139c"} {"question" "q-331744" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eb4d28ad52ee2323cc5eba3288092cabe973f18909117f96787114c62ba4296e"} {"question" "q-331744" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7543c24749d210763776b1dd36828a705b7ca0c0e31051b46dad1ebd313139c"} {"question" "q-331744" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7543c24749d210763776b1dd36828a705b7ca0c0e31051b46dad1ebd313139c"} {"question" "q-331744" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043781/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7543c24749d210763776b1dd36828a705b7ca0c0e31051b46dad1ebd313139c"}] "usage" {"q-331744" [] "q-8352" [{"kind" "presentations" "entity" 17592186283049} {"kind" "responses" "entity" 17592186283049}]} "basis" 1622 "retractions" [[17592186088363 :question/worked-solution "The equation $yy^{\\prime}+x=y$ has a derivative coefficient depending on the unknown $y$. Dividing by $y$, where possible, gives $y^{\\prime}=1-x/y$, which is nonlinear in $y$. The remaining choices can all be rearranged into $y^{\\prime}+P(x)y=Q(x)$; a complicated function of $x$ alone is allowed."] [17592186088363 :question/difficulty 17592186045463] [17592186088081 :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n1. $y^{\\prime } - xy = 0$\n2. $y^{\\prime } - xy = y$\n3. $y^{\\prime } - xy = x^{3}$"] [17592186088081 :question/worked-solution "An equation is inhomogeneous when its forcing function is not identically zero after collecting all $y$ terms on the left. Equation I has zero forcing. Equation II becomes $y^{\\prime}-(x+1)y=0$ and also has zero forcing. Equation III has forcing $x^3$, which is not identically zero. Therefore only III is inhomogeneous."] [17592186088081 :question/difficulty 17592186045463]] "immutable_answers" [17592186085392 17592186085393 17592186085394 17592186085395 17592186085396 17592186087109 17592186087110 17592186087111 17592186087112 17592186087113] "immutable_fields" [[17592186085391 :answer-field/correct] [17592186085391 :answer-field/id] [17592186085391 :answer-field/key] [17592186085391 :answer-field/type] [17592186087108 :answer-field/correct] [17592186087108 :answer-field/id] [17592186087108 :answer-field/key] [17592186087108 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
